cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "ECOCIDE — What Did the Destruction of the Kakhovka Dam Do to Vegetation? Separating Flood, Reservoir and Irrigation Effects with Satellite Data"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/ECOCIDE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/ECOCIDE"
abstract: >-
  Code, analysis plans and results for a pre-registered, pixel-level test of
  the vegetation effects of the Kakhovka Dam destruction (6 June 2023) using
  MODIS NDVI (250 m, 2010-2024). Treatment is defined by exposure (flooded
  land, drained reservoir bed, canal-irrigated cropland) and comparisons are
  made inside the war zone, under a plan fixed before any data download and
  two registered revisions (the second with independent 30 m irrigation maps
  and terrain-based flood matching). No lasting loss of July-October greenness
  was detected on flooded land (except floodplain wetlands); about 1,500 km2
  of the former reservoir became vegetated by 2024; formerly canal-irrigated
  fields lost about a third of their summer greenness after the breach
  relative to never-irrigated cropland, a result the pre-registered rule names
  suggestive. Study 1 (Kherson Oblast vs Romanian counties, monthly
  Sentinel-2 NDVI) is retained as background.
  Zenodo v1.0.0 and EarthArXiv preprint v1 report an earlier, superseded
  analysis.
keywords:
  - pre-registration
  - MODIS
  - ecocide
  - remote sensing
  - causal inference
  - difference-in-differences
  - satellite evidence
  - Kakhovka Dam
  - NDVI
  - geospatial analysis
